Purpose of the changes: emulating the `/dev/binder` driver, the framebuffer,
input, networking and `ashmem` for the guest system, a trace of the guest on the
host clock (`guest_trace.c`), the importance of guest processes as the host
kernel sees it (`guest_oom.c`), running `#!` scripts and answering `execve` errors
the way the kernel does (`guest_script.c`), the kernel-like services a firmware's
own `/init` expects when it is run under the translator (device nodes, kernel
command line, journals for the stand: `guest_fwinit.c`), the SELinux “everything is
allowed” answers the same `/init` needs on Android 5.0 and newer (a selinuxfs directory,
process attributes, file labels, the peer context of a socket: `guest_selinux.c`; file labels alone,
without the rest of SELinux, for Android 7.x started by the stand itself), the guest's own `user.*`
extended attributes kept on the real file of the phone (`guest_xattr.h`), what the guest sees of the
stand: the names of its UNIX sockets as guest paths and the stand's own descriptors hidden from its
`/proc/self/fd` (patches 0093, 0094, `guest_proc.c`), the guest's own view of its process in `/proc` also
through `task/<thread>/` and `thread-self` (patch 0095, `guest_procpath.h`), a journal of
the signals one guest process sends to another — who, to whom, which signal (`guest_kill.c`), the
alarm device `/dev/alarm` of Android kept on the phone's timers (patch 0096, `guest_alarm.c`,
`guest_alarm_core.h`), the return from a signal handler into Thumb code (`linux-user/arm/signal.c`), the sessions of the emulated
touchscreen and their relay threads, which end as soon as the guest closes the device by any path and
greet the stand with a session token (`guest_input_pump.h`), plus working around the
ways Android behaves as a *host* (paths, permissions, identifiers, SELinux
restrictions). Every patch begins with a description of the symptom it exists
for. The numbers and both tables of this section are checked against the tree
before every release.

The declarations of the Linux binder ioctl interface in `qemu/src/binder_proto.h`
follow `include/uapi/linux/android/binder.h` of the Linux kernel (GPL-2.0 WITH
Linux-syscall-note); the rest of that file, and the “shim <-> binderd” protocol,
are ours.

