## 4. The application itself

The Android application (`tech.istratii.senses`, Kotlin) and its own libraries
are **a separate program**. The libraries in the package are:

* on the phone (arm64, `lib/arm64-v8a/`, built with the Android NDK):
  `libglbridge.so` (the GL bridge, loaded into the application),
  `libglserverd.so` (the GL server), `libbinderd.so` (`binderd`, the binder
  daemon), `libguestrun.so` (`guestrun`, the launcher of guest programs) and
  `libbtstub.so` (`btstub`, a stand-in for one binder service of the guest). They
  link dynamically only against the system libraries of the phone (libc, libm,
  libdl, liblog, libandroid, libEGL, libGLESv1/v2/v3). `libqemu.so` in the same
  directory is the modified QEMU of section 1 and is not covered by this
  paragraph;
* in the guest (ARM32, inside `assets/tools.payload`, copied by the application
  into the guest tree on the device): `libGLES.so`, `libGLESfollow.so`,
  `libashmemshim.so`, `noop`, `audio.primary.default.so`, `gralloc.default.so`,
  `hwcomposer.default.so` and `consumerir.default.so` (built without a C library
  of their own: they need nothing, or only the guest's own `libc.so`), the
  class file archive `hello.jar` and the generated property area `guest.props`.

QEMU is started from the application as a **separate process**
(`ProcessBuilder`); they communicate over sockets, command-line arguments and
files. No QEMU code is loaded into the application's process, and none of QEMU's
source is used in these libraries — they are written from scratch on raw system
calls and the system libraries listed above.

`binderd` and the translator talk over a socket using the protocol declared in
`qemu/src/binder_proto.h` (published under GPL-2.0-or-later); `binderd` contains
no QEMU code and is not part of QEMU.

